Subject: Fundamental ggvis behavior
Message-ID: <O1CBUMLFsr0.cs0yyZu0Rs0J@ggvis.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggvis/c/O1CBUMLFsr0/m/cs0yyZu0Rs0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This will successfully generate an the same plot:

library(ggvis)
mtcars %>% ggvis(~cyl) %>% layer_histograms()

mtcars %>% ggvis() %>% layer_histograms(~cyl)

This too is successful:

vis <- mtcars %>% ggvis()

vis %>% layer_histograms(~cyl)

But this is not:

vis <- mtcars %>% ggvis()

vis_verb <-layer_histograms(~cyl)  ## This fails with 'Error: Can't find prop x.update'

vis %>% vis_verb

nor is this:

vis <- mtcars %>% ggvis()

vis_verb <-function() {layer_histograms(~cyl)}

vis %>% vis_verb ## This fails w 'Error in vis_verb(.) : unused argument (.)'

Why do the latter two examples fail?  What am I missing?

Maybe this is more of a basic R question-- I would welcome being pointed in the right direction.  Thank you for any insight.

From u373beb702917b8b5 at groups.google.com  Tue May 12 02:16:08 2015
From: u373beb702917b8b5 at groups.google.com (Ista Zahn)
Date: Tue, 12 May 2015 02:16:08 +0000
Subject: Re: Fundamental ggvis behavior
Message-ID: <O1CBUMLFsr0.kmNiRLiuCx0J@ggvis.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <O1CBUMLFsr0.cs0yyZu0Rs0J@ggvis.groups.google.com>
References: <O1CBUMLFsr0.cs0yyZu0Rs0J@ggvis.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggvis/c/O1CBUMLFsr0/m/kmNiRLiuCx0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The short answer is that the first argument to the layer_* functions
is always a vis object (as created by ggvis). You're failing examples
don't give layer_histogram a vis so it doesn't know what to do. It's
admittedly hard to see that because of the piping; things become
clearer I think if you avoid it:

## these all work (notice that the first argument
## to layer_histograms is always a ggvis object):
layer_histograms(ggvis(mtcars, ~cyl))
layer_histograms(ggvis(mtcars), ~cyl)
vis <- ggvis(mtcars)
layer_histograms(vis, ~cyl)

## this doesn't work, because the first argument
## to layer_histogram is not a vis object produced
## by ggvis:
vis_verb <-layer_histograms(~cyl)

Hope this helps,
Ista

